He wanted to be as close
to nature as possible,

00:22:19.930 --> 00:22:23.410
so this almost naked man has this octopus

00:22:23.410 --> 00:22:25.420
just nestling on his chest!

00:22:25.420 --> 00:22:27.430
He didn't reach out, grab the octopus,

00:22:27.430 --> 00:22:29.670
and put him on his chest.
No, the octopus did it.

00:22:29.670 --> 00:22:31.410
He let the octopus instigate.

00:22:31.410 --> 00:22:32.430
Take the initiative.

00:22:32.430 --> 00:22:35.380
But you left out an
important point, which was

00:22:37.010 --> 00:22:37.930
he was filming it.

00:22:37.930 --> 00:22:40.400
Once he was like, "Oh, I'm
gonna start filming this thing.

00:22:40.400 --> 00:22:42.250
I'm gonna take my camera down there."

00:22:43.670 --> 00:22:45.580
He had developed this trust, this bond,

00:22:45.580 --> 00:22:49.320
and then it was following
him, and he dropped a lens

00:22:49.320 --> 00:22:53.100
off of the camera, and
it spooked the octopus,

00:22:53.100 --> 00:22:55.590
and it did not go back to its den.

00:22:55.590 --> 00:22:56.870
So the next day, he goes back.

00:22:56.870 --> 00:22:58.293
The thing is not in its den.

00:22:59.967 --> 00:23:01.390
He goes back the next
day, it's not in its den.

00:23:01.390 --> 00:23:03.870
He's like, "Oh, crap, I've
ruined this relationship.

00:23:03.870 --> 00:23:04.920
We had built this trust,"

00:23:04.920 --> 00:23:06.220
and that
You broken up.

00:23:07.110 --> 00:23:09.250
was the first time I cried.

00:23:09.250 --> 00:23:11.410
I was already crying when he had the bond,

00:23:11.410 --> 00:23:12.420
and then he broke the bond.

00:23:12.420 --> 00:23:15.940
Oh, so you didn't cry when he successfully

00:23:15.940 --> 00:23:18.240
tracked her down and reconnected?

00:23:18.240 --> 00:23:19.330
Oh, no, I cried again then.

00:23:19.330 --> 00:23:20.163
That's number two?

00:23:20.163 --> 00:23:22.230
Yeah.
Well then, was number three,

00:23:22.230 --> 00:23:24.390
I mean-
It was at least three times.

00:23:24.390 --> 00:23:27.090
I mean, the big point of horror

00:23:27.090 --> 00:23:29.130
was him filming this shark

00:23:29.130 --> 00:23:32.360
Getting attacked.
attack her

00:23:32.360 --> 00:23:35.860
and rip off one of her arms

00:23:35.860 --> 00:23:40.860
and just eat it, and then
it gets to that boundary

00:23:41.190 --> 00:23:43.240
because he didn't stop it from happening,

00:23:43.240 --> 00:23:47.310
but then he says, "I couldn't resist,"

00:23:47.310 --> 00:23:48.830
basically, he couldn't resist helping,

00:23:48.830 --> 00:23:52.410
so he, you know, she's in, she's holed up

00:23:52.410 --> 00:23:53.243
in her-
She makes it back

00:23:53.243 --> 00:23:54.285
to the den.
Yeah, on her own.

00:23:54.285 --> 00:23:55.230
With seven limbs.

00:23:55.230 --> 00:23:56.940
He's tempted to help, but he doesn't help.

00:23:56.940 --> 00:23:59.930
He just films her going back to her den,

00:23:59.930 --> 00:24:03.700
limping with one less arm,
which has her brain it it.

00:24:03.700 --> 00:24:05.470
Yeah, lost a part of her brain.

00:24:05.470 --> 00:24:08.720
And then she's, like, she's
too weak to even change color.

00:24:08.720 --> 00:24:09.910
Yeah, she just turns white.

00:24:09.910 --> 00:24:14.910
And so, but he did crack open shells.

00:24:15.687 --> 00:24:16.520
Like some mussels or something.

00:24:16.520 --> 00:24:20.450
Mussels or something, and serve it to her

00:24:20.450 --> 00:24:22.060
to have some nourishment.

00:24:22.060 --> 00:24:23.080
And she took it.

00:24:23.080 --> 00:24:25.740
She took it, but he said,
"I don't think it did

00:24:25.740 --> 00:24:27.530
much good," but I think he
might've been saying that

00:24:27.530 --> 00:24:32.337
because he crossed his own
boundary, which I'm like,

00:24:32.337 --> 00:24:35.450
"Dude, you know, when
you're in a relationship

00:24:35.450 --> 00:24:37.410
with an animal, all bets are off."

00:24:37.410 --> 00:24:39.540
Like, it's not a pet, but

00:24:41.660 --> 00:24:42.857
there was a bond there,

00:24:42.857 --> 00:24:46.070
and he freaking, yeah,
I totally understand

00:24:46.070 --> 00:24:49.190
that he had to, like, do
what he could to help,

00:24:49.190 --> 00:24:54.190
but it seemed like a little
too little, too late,

00:24:54.370 --> 00:24:58.170
until she freaking grew her arm back.

00:24:58.170 --> 00:25:00.980
Did you, I didn't, I forgot
that that was possible.

00:25:00.980 --> 00:25:02.690
I didn't know it was possible.

00:25:02.690 --> 00:25:03.840
My jaw dropped.

00:25:03.840 --> 00:25:07.580
She grew the arm back, and
so she had eight limbs again,

00:25:07.580 --> 00:25:09.040
and she kinda came back to life,

00:25:09.040 --> 00:25:11.220
and again, so he lost contact with her,

00:25:11.220 --> 00:25:15.500
tracked her down, learned
how to track her movements

00:25:15.500 --> 00:25:18.273
by looking at the sand on
the bottom of the ocean,

00:25:19.260 --> 00:25:22.040
reunites with her, watches
her get attacked by the shark.

00:25:22.040 --> 00:25:24.940
She loses the limb, and then
she gets back in business,

00:25:24.940 --> 00:25:27.261
and then sort of the, it goes on

00:25:27.261 --> 00:25:28.550
and on and on.
The relationship continues.

00:25:28.550 --> 00:25:29.433
And then,

00:25:31.290 --> 00:25:35.410
lo and behold, the same
dang gone shark comes back,

00:25:35.410 --> 00:25:37.730
or one of the sharks
that hunts at this reef,

00:25:37.730 --> 00:25:42.690
comes back, and this point,
this is when my mind started-

00:25:42.690 --> 00:25:45.610
I was like, "Am I
experiencing reality correctly

00:25:45.610 --> 00:25:46.443
at this point?"

00:25:46.443 --> 00:25:48.370
Yeah.
Because the octopus

00:25:48.370 --> 00:25:52.000
has the ability to pick up

00:25:52.000 --> 00:25:53.700
all these shells

00:25:54.560 --> 00:25:58.560
and ball itself up and shield itself.

00:25:58.560 --> 00:26:00.900
It looks like a soccer ball that's made

00:26:00.900 --> 00:26:03.703
out of all these shells that it's holding,

00:26:04.620 --> 00:26:05.880
and there's a name for that as well.

00:26:05.880 --> 00:26:10.840
I can't remember what it
was, and the shark comes up.

00:26:10.840 --> 00:26:15.630
I do not think that this

00:26:15.630 --> 00:26:19.030
behavior was ever documented
before he observed it.

00:26:19.030 --> 00:26:20.580
'Cause he says he remembered seeing that.

00:26:20.580 --> 00:26:22.810
The first time he ever came
in contact with the octopus,

00:26:22.810 --> 00:26:25.340
it was in that state of this
soccer ball with the shells,

00:26:25.340 --> 00:26:27.180
and he was, like, "What
the hell is this thing?"

00:26:27.180 --> 00:26:29.574
And then he realized it's an
octopus protecting itself.

00:26:29.574 --> 00:26:31.173
And then there's, like,
epic battle with a shark

00:26:31.173 --> 00:26:34.630
that still is trying to eat
the soccer ball of shells.

00:26:34.630 --> 00:26:37.940
And then, okay, two things,
two remarkable things happen

00:26:37.940 --> 00:26:39.080
during the shark attack.

00:26:39.080 --> 00:26:44.010
The first thing that
happens is that once this,

00:26:44.010 --> 00:26:46.510
the shark picks up the octopus
and is shaking it around

00:26:46.510 --> 00:26:48.170
'cause it realizes, "Okay,
this isn't a soccer ball.

00:26:48.170 --> 00:26:51.200
This is an octopus," but
the octopus gets away

00:26:51.200 --> 00:26:54.790
and swims out of the
water, gets on the shore

00:26:54.790 --> 00:26:56.280
on a rock,

00:26:56.280 --> 00:26:57.890
and eventually has to
get back in the water

00:26:57.890 --> 00:26:59.640
because it can't stay out too long,

00:26:59.640 --> 00:27:03.150
and at that point, it
gets on the shark's back

00:27:03.150 --> 00:27:05.620
and rides the shark because it knows

00:27:05.620 --> 00:27:07.810
that the shark can't eat
it as long as it's riding

00:27:07.810 --> 00:27:09.600
on it like it's a freaking horse.

00:27:09.600 --> 00:27:13.930
But it rode it as a soccer ball of shells.

00:27:13.930 --> 00:27:15.830
Yeah, right, it kept itself in the shells

00:27:15.830 --> 00:27:16.663
while it did this.

00:27:16.663 --> 00:27:18.690
So that the shark

00:27:18.690 --> 00:27:21.700
was totally confused, and
then when it brushed up

00:27:21.700 --> 00:27:24.600
against some coral,

00:27:24.600 --> 00:27:27.640
it just, ever so

00:27:27.640 --> 00:27:32.490
normally, it just brushed
itself off and floated away.

00:27:32.490 --> 00:27:34.170
This thing is so freaking smart,

00:27:34.170 --> 00:27:36.010
and that's why you observe this thing

00:27:36.010 --> 00:27:39.800
every day for a year, and
you're making this bond with it,

00:27:39.800 --> 00:27:43.770
and you're saying, "Okay,
I'm now going to film

00:27:45.010 --> 00:27:48.110
my best ocean friend getting eaten alive."

00:27:48.110 --> 00:27:50.750
How much do you think was, of the footage

00:27:50.750 --> 00:27:53.190
that we were seeing in the documentary,

00:27:53.190 --> 00:27:56.130
was the footage that was
captured in the moment

00:27:56.130 --> 00:27:59.180
when it happened, and how
much was, like, filler,

00:27:59.180 --> 00:28:01.460
like, filling it, going
back and filming later

00:28:01.460 --> 00:28:02.500
to make it make sense?

00:28:02.500 --> 00:28:04.210
Like pickups, like movie pickups?

00:28:04.210 --> 00:28:06.830
Like, I do think the
octopus is smart enough

00:28:06.830 --> 00:28:08.550
to comply to pickups.

00:28:08.550 --> 00:28:09.740
Like, "Hey, can you get on the back

00:28:09.740 --> 00:28:11.730
of that pyjama shark again?"

00:28:11.730 --> 00:28:14.700
And you know, it's like, "We're gonna look

00:28:14.700 --> 00:28:15.820
at the continuity photos.

00:28:15.820 --> 00:28:17.040
You're gonna get the same shells

00:28:17.040 --> 00:28:18.350
in the same place."
'Cause every single thing

00:28:18.350 --> 00:28:20.130
that would happen,
I think he captured

00:28:20.130 --> 00:28:21.120
all of it,
they would, he had

00:28:21.120 --> 00:28:22.007
incredible shots of it!

00:28:22.007 --> 00:28:25.630
And I think that was a level of obsession

00:28:25.630 --> 00:28:27.990
that I totally understand.

00:28:27.990 --> 00:28:30.410
It's like, again, you're making this bond,

00:28:30.410 --> 00:28:33.870
and then you don't know what's
going to happen every day,

00:28:33.870 --> 00:28:35.730
and it's like, "Is this
the day that I come back,

00:28:35.730 --> 00:28:39.370
and the octopus is just
not there, you know,

00:28:39.370 --> 00:28:41.120
has been attacked and is just gone?

00:28:42.450 --> 00:28:44.780
Am I gonna have to track it again?"

00:28:44.780 --> 00:28:47.010
But that's why it's so amazing to me

00:28:47.010 --> 00:28:52.000
that he didn't intervene
except in that one breach,

00:28:52.000 --> 00:28:55.810
you know, with feeding,
and then it, you know,

00:28:55.810 --> 00:28:58.160
comes back, and it's like
there's another octopus,

00:28:58.160 --> 00:29:01.300
they're mating, they've mated,
and then it's like he knows

00:29:01.300 --> 00:29:02.610
that she's gonna die at that point,

00:29:02.610 --> 00:29:04.737
so he's coming back every day to see.

00:29:04.737 --> 00:29:06.430
That's the end of the lifecycle.

00:29:06.430 --> 00:29:09.630
Yeah, only a year, something that smart.

00:29:09.630 --> 00:29:12.230
At what point in the process do you think

00:29:12.230 --> 00:29:17.230
that the inner filmmaker,
there's a spark sometimes,

00:29:17.410 --> 00:29:20.150
especially when you're
doing documentary film,

00:29:20.150 --> 00:29:21.163
there's a spark.

00:29:22.444 --> 00:29:24.100
We don't have a whole lot
of experience with this,

00:29:24.100 --> 00:29:25.827
but I do remember it when we were making

00:29:25.827 --> 00:29:27.540
"Looking for Ms.
Locklear," which is really

00:29:27.540 --> 00:29:28.750
the only documentary we've ever made,

00:29:28.750 --> 00:29:33.350
full-length documentary, and once

00:29:33.350 --> 00:29:37.640
the search for our teacher
became much larger, it became

00:29:37.640 --> 00:29:41.090
about the struggle of the Lumbee people,

00:29:41.090 --> 00:29:43.940
and, like, we found
ourselves in Washington, DC,

00:29:43.940 --> 00:29:46.860
at a hearing, filming John McCain

00:29:48.320 --> 00:29:51.330
talk about, you know, and
the Indian Affairs Committee.

00:29:51.330 --> 00:29:53.880
There was this moment,
somewhere along the lines,

00:29:53.880 --> 00:29:57.190
where I was, like, "We've
got something here," right?

00:29:57.190 --> 00:30:01.560
He was, at what point do
you think he understood

00:30:01.560 --> 00:30:03.860
the potential for what he was creating?

00:30:03.860 --> 00:30:07.200
I mean, he was a nature documentarian.

00:30:07.200 --> 00:30:10.000
I think it had to be pretty early,

00:30:10.000 --> 00:30:12.410
and I do think that was the factor

00:30:12.410 --> 00:30:15.080
because I'm not that familiar with him,

00:30:15.080 --> 00:30:19.830
but I have to believe
that there's these rules

00:30:19.830 --> 00:30:21.870
of you don't intervene, you know.

00:30:21.870 --> 00:30:25.390
Well, there's the famous
photo that won a prize

00:30:25.390 --> 00:30:27.490
of some sort, whatever the photo prize is,

00:30:29.500 --> 00:30:31.223
of the starving child.
Right.

00:30:32.640 --> 00:30:33.850
I don't even remember what it was from.

00:30:33.850 --> 00:30:36.290
So there's a journalistic
principle that applies

00:30:36.290 --> 00:30:38.550
to nature as well that's like-
Well, and just to complete

00:30:38.550 --> 00:30:41.890
the thought, though, there's
a picture of a starving child

00:30:41.890 --> 00:30:45.790
that was walking to get
food, and this journalist,

00:30:45.790 --> 00:30:47.670
photojournalist, took
a picture of the child

00:30:47.670 --> 00:30:51.120
but did not assist the child,
and I think the story goes

00:30:51.120 --> 00:30:55.110
that the child died, and when
people found out about that,

00:30:55.110 --> 00:30:57.310
they were like, "Well,
okay, what's more important:

00:30:57.310 --> 00:31:01.010
your journalistic principles
or the life of this child?"

00:31:01.010 --> 00:31:04.060
This isn't a child, this is an
octopus we're talking about,

00:31:04.060 --> 00:31:06.130
but there is this sort of commitment

00:31:06.130 --> 00:31:08.250
to these journalistic
principles that he went

00:31:08.250 --> 00:31:12.420
into the process knowing that,
like, "If I'm gonna make this

00:31:12.420 --> 00:31:16.730
into something, I understand
the type of criticism

00:31:16.730 --> 00:31:18.510
that I'm gonna receive, especially

00:31:18.510 --> 00:31:21.563
when it's an animal,
for intervening," right?

00:31:22.880 --> 00:31:25.370
And he really, he held strong.

00:31:25.370 --> 00:31:27.650
I couldn't have done
that, and I found myself

00:31:27.650 --> 00:31:30.590
being angry with him, when the octopus

00:31:30.590 --> 00:31:33.680
was in that epic battle as a boulder.

00:31:33.680 --> 00:31:34.513
'Cause he coulda just reached out

00:31:34.513 --> 00:31:35.770
and, like, slapped a shark.

00:31:35.770 --> 00:31:39.310
But maybe that's a good
point to analyze because

00:31:40.960 --> 00:31:44.660
an extraordinary thing was
documented that happened.

00:31:44.660 --> 00:31:45.806
You know, it's like-

00:31:45.806 --> 00:31:47.150
And you don't wanna see a
human hand come in there

00:31:47.150 --> 00:31:49.350
and, like, thump the shark away.

00:31:49.350 --> 00:31:52.170
Yeah, this is not the story, you know,

00:31:53.820 --> 00:31:55.560
he was presenting her story,

00:31:55.560 --> 00:31:58.980
not their story, but really?

00:31:58.980 --> 00:32:01.200
I mean, when the directors
took the footage,

00:32:01.200 --> 00:32:04.360
they presented his story,
but it was more about how,

00:32:04.360 --> 00:32:07.478
you know, he had a son, and
there was a connection to,

00:32:07.478 --> 00:32:09.378
it was what did the octopus teach him?

00:32:11.230 --> 00:32:14.720
And there was, you know,
his takeaway was more

00:32:14.720 --> 00:32:17.780
of a, that we're all connected.

00:32:17.780 --> 00:32:21.670
Right, well, and it
raises a larger question,

00:32:21.670 --> 00:32:23.640
which I think we'll get
into as we begin to talk

00:32:23.640 --> 00:32:25.640
about some of these other scenarios,

00:32:25.640 --> 00:32:30.310
which is what part of
this incredible connection

00:32:30.310 --> 00:32:32.240
between this animal
that's more intelligent

00:32:32.240 --> 00:32:35.190
than I ever understood, you know,

00:32:35.190 --> 00:32:37.730
and I think about, you know,
my relationship with Barbara,

00:32:37.730 --> 00:32:40.233
your relationship with Jade,
who is currently in your lap,

00:32:41.240 --> 00:32:44.780
and definitely, I attribute human emotions

00:32:44.780 --> 00:32:48.090
and human instincts to Barbara.

00:32:48.090 --> 00:32:49.900
Like, when Barbara looks at me with a,

00:32:49.900 --> 00:32:51.940
she gives me a facial expression

00:32:52.780 --> 00:32:55.780
that may be like, well,
she's confused right now,

00:32:55.780 --> 00:32:59.270
or she is annoyed right now,
or she's happy right now,

00:32:59.270 --> 00:33:01.270
and again, a lotta that is me projecting

00:33:02.400 --> 00:33:04.900
a human understanding onto a dog's face,

00:33:04.900 --> 00:33:06.800
even though we do know that dogs

00:33:08.110 --> 00:33:10.540
since, you know, being
domesticated, have developed

00:33:10.540 --> 00:33:12.440
these things that only exist with them

00:33:12.440 --> 00:33:14.060
and don't exist with
wolves, like the ability

00:33:14.060 --> 00:33:17.183
to make sustained eye
contact with a human,

00:33:18.480 --> 00:33:20.530
to communicate via eye contact.

00:33:20.530 --> 00:33:22.883
That's not something that
would happen with a wolf,

00:33:23.990 --> 00:33:26.190
but even so, it makes sense

00:33:26.190 --> 00:33:28.380
that there is some sort
of emotional connection.

00:33:28.380 --> 00:33:30.540
Like, I feel like when
I'm doing my stretching

00:33:30.540 --> 00:33:33.490
in the morning, and Barbara
comes and gets on my chest

00:33:33.490 --> 00:33:35.230
and, like, puts her head on my face

00:33:35.230 --> 00:33:37.580
or licks me or whatever, I don't think

00:33:37.580 --> 00:33:38.910
that she's just, "Well, I'm doing this

00:33:38.910 --> 00:33:40.430
because you're gonna feed me later."

00:33:40.430 --> 00:33:42.500
That might be a part of it, but I do think

00:33:42.500 --> 00:33:44.963
that there is an
emotional component to it,

00:33:48.150 --> 00:33:52.090
but how much of this was an understanding

00:33:52.090 --> 00:33:54.110
that this octopus had?

00:33:54.110 --> 00:33:56.110
Which is like, and again,
they wouldn't put it

00:33:56.110 --> 00:33:58.990
in human terms, but, like,
how much of it do you think

00:33:58.990 --> 00:34:03.990
was, insofar as an octopus
can consider a human a friend,

00:34:04.010 --> 00:34:05.650
that was what was going on?

00:34:05.650 --> 00:34:07.963
It seemed that way. It really did.

00:34:09.120 --> 00:34:12.223
I mean, like the, he wasn't-

00:34:14.940 --> 00:34:18.450
She, the octopus, was not
getting anything from him,

00:34:18.450 --> 00:34:21.370
except for that one little
time, he was not feeding her.

00:34:21.370 --> 00:34:23.573
He was not doing anything except,

00:34:26.180 --> 00:34:28.193
like, being interesting.

00:34:29.040 --> 00:34:33.330
You know, she was interested,
and there was a connection.

00:34:33.330 --> 00:34:34.910
There was a form of relationship,

00:34:34.910 --> 00:34:37.140
like curling up on his
chest and stuff like that,

